-# February 17 Myers interview records a request for NORAD’s analysis
+# October 2003 archive capture of the FAA fact sheet
 
-November 8, 2026 Eastern. B129 / PD01–PD04. The National Archives’ declassified copy of the Commission’s Myers interview memo records a request that predates the February 20 letter about after-action reports. The memo supplies a date, two offices and a subject for finding the underlying analysis and any follow-up. Its first page identifies the interview and gives the September 29, 2014 declassification date, matching the ISCAP release table. [Interview memo, p. 1](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2012-047-doc4.pdf#page=1) · [ISCAP release table, entry 2012-047-doc4](https://www.archives.gov/declassification/iscap/decision-table)
+November 8, 2026 Eastern. B129 / PD01 / PD07 and NR208. An **October 25, 2003** Wayback capture of the FAA’s August 12, 2002 fact sheet carries the same 09:24 Eastern Daylight Time account as the previously held **June 26, 2004** capture. The two replay bodies are byte-identical at 6,933 bytes. The newly recovered capture is from `www1.faa.gov` with lowercase `newsroom`. The June capture is from `www.faa.gov` with `Newsroom`. Both dates follow the May 2003 Commission answer. The fact sheet’s printed August 2002 date and the archived capture dates describe different events. [October 2003 replay](https://web.archive.org/web/20031025224053id_/http://www1.faa.gov:80/newsroom/factsheets/2002/factsheets_020812.htm) · [June 2004 replay](https://web.archive.org/web/20040626232523id_/http://www.faa.gov:80/Newsroom/factsheets/2002/factsheets_020812.htm)
 
-On **p. 6**, the Commission memo says Myers had seen NORAD after-action “analysis” that included information about FAA notifications. It says he would not call it an after-action report. It also records that he was asked to determine whether NORAD had conducted an after-action review and to provide it to the Commission if so. On **p. 7**, the memo records his commitment to call Eberhart and obtain analytic work after a discussion of whether Flight 93 could have reached its target. The first passage concerns an after-action review and the second follows a narrower discussion. Keep both scopes open until an actual response identifies the records. These are staff notes of the interview. The date Myers saw the analysis, the call and any delivery require other records. [Interview memo, p. 6](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2012-047-doc4.pdf#page=6) · [Commitment, p. 7](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2012-047-doc4.pdf#page=7)
+In both earlier bodies, HTML line 104 says FAA notified NEADS at 09:24 about the suspected hijacking of American 77 and that FAA and NORAD opened a line to discuss American 77 and United 93. In the already held November 1, 2004 capture, lines 109–111 give **09:20** for an information-sharing line with government and military participants and show an **October 21, 2004** revision label. The replacement changes the event described, its stated time, and the specificity of the participants. Both descriptions remain preserved. Their times cannot be compared as measurements of a single event without the original call records. [November 2004 replay](https://web.archive.org/web/20041101003838id_/http://faa.gov/newsroom/factsheets/2002/factsheets_020812.htm)
 
-The **February 20 Commission letter** described a broad request under DoD Request No. 1, item 10. The **March 16 DoD response** applied a military-report definition and pointed to weekly indexes. The NORAD enclosure distinguished a single comprehensive report from a series it said had been provided. The February 17 memo adds a particular analysis and a specific follow-up channel to that production trail. Myers’s terminology leaves open whether his materials fell within the narrower March 16 definition. The titles of interim reports alone cannot join those bodies to what he saw. [March correspondence and original locators](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/3324e3ae36db9189a79658db51ef04acb6663219/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-march16-after-action-correspondence-2026-11-08/README.md)
+DOT OIG reports that it raised the fact sheet error with FAA in October 2004 and that FAA deleted the erroneous wording. It separately reports a September 2004 approach about the other FAA page. The October 2003 body documents the earlier wording on the fact sheet. The captured date and revision label leave the exact approval and posting times open. The November body independently establishes the captured replacement wording. The two identical earlier bodies represent one version and cannot independently corroborate the operational claim. [DOT OIG report, printed p. 8](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=8)
 
-The final Commission report’s chapter 1 notes **238–240** identify aircraft performance, radar and operating assumptions for arrival estimates, interviews for pilot awareness, and a channel recording for the authority question. Those citations identify inputs to specific parts of the Commission’s analysis. They do not identify a return to the February 17 request. [Commission report, ch. 1 notes 238–240](https://www.911commission.gov/report/911Report_Notes.htm)
+The search began with a May 25, 2003 bibliographic reference to a differently formatted FAA address. The archive’s availability endpoint returned no closest capture for the three tested spellings. The canonical path queries for August 12, 2002 and May 25, 2003 both returned the same October 2003 locator. Neither query date establishes an archived page on that day, and the citation provides no body for the alternate path. The replay returned HTTP 200, `text/html`, and a Memento date of **Sat, 25 Oct 2003 22:40:53 GMT**. The original exchange and WARC bytes were outside this check. [Bibliographic reference](https://www.encyclopedia.com/politics/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/september-11-terrorist-attacks-united-states) · [Source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json)
 
-- [ ] Seek a dated February 17 follow-up from the Chairman’s office to NORAD, an Eberhart response, and the underlying analysis Myers said he saw. Match sender, recipient, subject, version and attachments against a Commission receipt, dated review note or index entry.
-- [ ] Keep the after-action review request, the Flight 93 discussion and the February 20 DoD request as separate scopes in that comparison. A responsive cover or attachment could establish whether the same analysis answered multiple requests.
-- [ ] Retain the weekly delivery and Commission intake records, March chronology letter and receipt, corrected FAA answer and recipient acknowledgment, signed administrative decision and recommendation dispositions as separate open records.
